Subject: [PATCH 09872/11362] Withdraw the REQ-6710 prevalence claim; re-run
 all mutations uncontaminated

Three team-lead rulings, applied. Source and spec changes for these already
reached HEAD via the conductor's sweep; this commit lands the record.

1. MUTATION PROOFS RE-RUN FROM SCRATCH; THE EARLIER SET IS VOID. Two mutation
   harnesses had been writing the same three source files in the same checkout
   with no lock. A RED I attributed to my own deleted pattern could equally have
   been the other agent's mutation live in the file at that moment -- which would
   make a decorative change look load-bearing. That is the exact inverse of the
   M3/M4 error, and it is undetectable by re-reading my own code. So no earlier
   reading is carried forward.

   All eleven mutations were re-run in an isolated `git worktree` copy. One
   detail was load-bearing and nearly cost the whole exercise: the venv resolves
   `carnot` to the MAIN checkout, so worktree mutations would have been silently
   inert and every mutation would have read GREEN -- a proof run that proves
   nothing while looking clean. Verified by import, then pinned with PYTHONPATH.

     M1  held-out site sets outer `skipped`                      RED
     M2  exception site sets outer `skipped`                     RED
     M3  round record carries `heldout_accuracy`                 RED
     M4  round record carries `accepted_by_heldout_verifier`     RED
     M5  `_round_digest` keeps the per-round record              RED
     M6  chat channel split accumulated                          RED
     M7  `_channel_totals_delta` differences the counters        RED
     M8  loop-side `channels_before` read                        RED
     M9  no-plan fall-through claims its cause                   RED
     M10 `_keep_head_and_tail` keeps the last round              RED
     M11 harness `_channel_delta` clamps                         RED

   Baseline GREEN at 14 tests. Zero `MUTATED` markers in the three files before
   and after the run, and zero in the main checkout immediately before staging
   -- grepped, and all three files re-parsed.

2. THE PREVALENCE FIGURE IS WITHDRAWN, NOT CORRECTED. I quoted "9 of 18" from
   the brief, then "corrected" it to a measured "9 of 120". Both are withdrawn.
   `no_reachable_plan_after_refinement` is ALSO the correct label for a genuine
   no-plan attempt, so counting that string cannot separate a real planning
   failure from a masked dynamics failure -- no denominator makes it meaningful.
   Measuring it more carefully was the wrong move; the counter cannot answer the
   question at all. The defect is now stated from the code path, and the
   per-round record this REQ adds is what makes the prevalence measurable for
   the first time. The withdrawn figures are named in ops/status.md rather than
   deleted, so the record shows what was believed and when it changed.

3. `plan_reaches_goal` STAYS None WHEN ABSENT. Reasoning recorded at the harness
   read site: absent means the planner never ran, False means it ran and found
   nothing. Defaulting it to False would rebuild the masking defect inside the
   instrument built to expose it.

PRECEDENT WORTH RECORDING: the mutation discipline caught its own author. M3 and
M4 stayed GREEN on deletion, which is what "decorative" means here, and the two
row writes I had added were duplicates of writes the round's `row.update(...)`
block already made. I had asserted the opposite to the team lead, who confirmed
it using a grep with the same subscript-only blind spot as mine -- two greps
agreeing is not corroboration when both carry the same defect. The proof, not
the review, is what found it.

Mutation harnesses must not run in this checkout: the conductor sweeps with
verification skipped on its own schedule, and a mutated line that is still valid
Python passes every hook.

ONE MUTATION MARKER DELETED DELIBERATELY, per the guard's documented resolution.
It named exactly one path, ops/changelog.md, and recorded it as unattributed. It
is this commit's own authored append: the marker was armed by the verification
pytest run, and the append landed after that snapshot, so an edit looked like a
test rewrite. Not test damage. Nothing was reverted with git checkout, and the
conductor's in-flight results/experiment_6586 file was left untouched.

Verified: 152 tests pass across the 10 suites touching this work. ruff check and
ruff format clean. Nothing under results/** modified or staged by me.

